# SEO business context

Use this reference after confirming the target site. Reuse the user's supplied
business context and previous reports; do not silently inspect credential files
or launch a separate authentication flow.

## Establish the decisions the audit must support

Identify the product or service, target regions and languages, paying customer,
main conversion, and highest-value offers. Ask only the missing questions that
would change prioritization. Label inferred details as hypotheses, with the page
or query evidence that suggested them.

## Connect search demand to the business

Separate brand terms, product terms, problems, comparison intent, and informational
research. For each promising cluster, record the audience, intent, existing page,
conversion path, evidence period, and the commercial reason to prioritize it.
High impressions alone do not justify a topic outside the site's actual offer.

Check whether search visitors match the stated buyer and geography. Distinguish
audience mismatch from poor on-page conversion. Use GA4 or other connected
measurement only when available; Search Console clicks do not prove conversions.

## Build a working brief

Summarize brand terms, audience hypotheses, differentiators supported by public
evidence, priority offers, preferred tone, content constraints, and success metric.
Return the brief to the user or save it at an authorized destination. Preserve
manual context and note unresolved assumptions for later content handoffs.
